WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.070
So, if yesterday's theme of the day was the why,

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.350
today's theme is the how.

3
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.310
And I thought we could start by talking about how not to.

4
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.040
And actually, why is it that many AI projects fail when businesses

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.630
try to implement them, and what you can do about it.

6
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:25.910
This is going to be useful.

7
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:33.830
So, first of all, a little bit about the statistics.

8
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.440
We know that Gartner has stated that 85% of AI

9
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.830
projects fail to deliver any business value.

10
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.480
Boston Consulting Group has said that 70% of failures are people and

11
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.030
processes, not even technology.

12
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.790
And Dun & Bradstreet claimed that only around 5% of businesses

13
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.390
have their data ready for expanding AI at scale.

14
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.470
So, all of these failures have nothing to do with the technology.

15
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.440
They all have to do with the people, the processes,

16
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.630
the business. And they fail because of what I think are five

17
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.760
specific, yet fixable mistakes.

18
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.960
And we're going to go through these five mistakes in the next few

19
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.870
minutes and talk about them.

20
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.110
So, the first problem is that you're solving the wrong problem.

21
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.470
You solved something, it's just not the right something.

22
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.960
When you go into an organization and you identify what the use

23
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.080
case is, where the place to use AI is,

24
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.590
a lot of times you might get

25
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.440
ideas, but they are the wrong ideas, and they don't actually lead to

26
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.200
value. For example, at Matrix, which is a consulting company that

27
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.190
does AI solutions, data transformation, we went into a client,

28
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.920
which was an HR department, and we brainstormed with them about what

29
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.760
AI solutions, what their challenges are,

30
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.932
what their needs are, and they came up and they said,

31
00:02:01.932 --> 00:02:03.162
"Can you build us a chatbot?

32
00:02:03.932 --> 00:02:08.212
An HR chatbot." Now, the most important word in an implementer's

33
00:02:08.212 --> 00:02:10.242
vocabulary is the word why.

34
00:02:10.852 --> 00:02:13.172
The question why. Because when they came and said,

35
00:02:13.172 --> 00:02:15.932
"We want a chatbot." We asked, "Okay, why?

36
00:02:15.932 --> 00:02:19.282
What is the problem that you're trying to solve with a chatbot?"

37
00:02:20.132 --> 00:02:22.852
And they said, "Well, employees are asking all these questions about

38
00:02:22.852 --> 00:02:25.172
holidays, payrolls, etc.

39
00:02:25.172 --> 00:02:28.292
And they should be on the intranet, but nobody can find it,

40
00:02:28.292 --> 00:02:31.922
so we want them to have a conversation to be able to solve it."

41
00:02:33.172 --> 00:02:37.012
And we identified the problem here is that the information

42
00:02:37.012 --> 00:02:40.442
was not accessible. There was an issue of content discoverability.

43
00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:44.812
It wasn't necessarily that you had to jump all the way to a chatbot,

44
00:02:44.812 --> 00:02:48.732
to AI to solve it. You just had to maybe fix the intranet,

45
00:02:48.732 --> 00:02:52.602
their internal website, and make it more discoverable.

46
00:02:53.932 --> 00:02:58.132
Right? So, the chatbot here was not actually necessarily the best

47
00:02:58.132 --> 00:03:01.772
answer. In fact, when we sat with them and we talked about the

48
00:03:01.772 --> 00:03:04.242
different use cases that they have and their challenges,

49
00:03:04.692 --> 00:03:10.212
we realized that there were much more valuable use cases

50
00:03:10.212 --> 00:03:12.612
that would bring the organization more value.

51
00:03:12.612 --> 00:03:16.452
When we identify these use cases, we think about them through a

52
00:03:16.452 --> 00:03:19.042
structured process of evaluation.

53
00:03:20.212 --> 00:03:26.132
Looking at their feasibility, how easy you can

54
00:03:26.132 --> 00:03:28.692
implement them. But then also their business value.

55
00:03:28.692 --> 00:03:30.812
Are they going to move the needle for the business?

56
00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:33.132
Is there going to be any impact in the business?

57
00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:36.852
And one of the use cases that we identified was that the HR

58
00:03:36.852 --> 00:03:41.082
employees had to create hundreds of letters every month.

59
00:03:41.492 --> 00:03:45.442
Whether it's around positions changing, status changes,

60
00:03:46.532 --> 00:03:52.372
termination, raise, getting more money, maybe changing your

61
00:03:52.372 --> 00:03:54.692
title. And all of these were done manually.

62
00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:57.212
They'd have to pull all this information from all these different

63
00:03:57.212 --> 00:04:00.052
systems, put them in a letter, write the letter.

64
00:04:00.052 --> 00:04:02.534
It was all manual. It had to go to approval,

65
00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:06.284
come back. There were many errors, human errors understandably.

66
00:04:06.814 --> 00:04:10.014
And we realized that actually the best solution for them,

67
00:04:10.014 --> 00:04:13.414
the problem that would make the most sense and would give

68
00:04:13.414 --> 00:04:17.294
them the most value, was to create some kind of AI-based letter

69
00:04:17.294 --> 00:04:21.364
creation tool that would create the initial draft.

70
00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:25.054
Rather than take days, it would take minutes,

71
00:04:25.054 --> 00:04:26.364
and they could work with that.

72
00:04:27.374 --> 00:04:30.254
So, it's very important, first of all, to identify the correct

73
00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:34.364
problem to solve where AI can really give you value.

74
00:04:36.414 --> 00:04:38.654
So, let's say you found the right problem.

75
00:04:38.654 --> 00:04:42.814
The second issue, the second reason projects fail, is because you give

76
00:04:42.814 --> 00:04:43.804
the wrong solution.

77
00:04:44.854 --> 00:04:47.164
Maybe it's the wrong tool or the wrong architecture.

78
00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:57.014
Organizations have different rationales for the solutions that they

79
00:04:57.014 --> 00:05:01.974
want. We had a client that waited 18 months for their ERP vendor's

80
00:05:01.974 --> 00:05:04.884
AI roadmap. There was a feature in the roadmap

81
00:05:06.374 --> 00:05:11.294
that they waited for rather than try to build their own tool.

82
00:05:11.294 --> 00:05:16.574
And it took us, with AI tools, 4 weeks to build a solution that did

83
00:05:16.574 --> 00:05:18.364
70% of what they needed.

84
00:05:18.894 --> 00:05:20.654
That was the thought here.

85
00:05:20.654 --> 00:05:26.414
Is that the innovation, even, you know, they say that perfect is

86
00:05:26.414 --> 00:05:29.894
the enemy of the good. You're not going to get the 100%,

87
00:05:29.894 --> 00:05:32.894
but if you can get 70% and start right away,

88
00:05:32.894 --> 00:05:36.204
it's worth the risk of doing it and moving forward.

89
00:05:36.974 --> 00:05:41.974
And so, some mistakes that organizations make when doing these

90
00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:45.454
AI projects. Waiting for the perfect vendor tool.

91
00:05:45.454 --> 00:05:49.974
Right? If the roadmap says Q3 and your problem exists today,

92
00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:53.204
it might be worth building that tool today.

93
00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:55.404
There's also the inverse problem

94
00:05:56.654 --> 00:06:00.124
that you rush to build something that you could just buy.

95
00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:03.804
If you build something, it's important to build for differentiation.

96
00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:08.375
If it is a specific use case, a specific situation that your

97
00:06:08.375 --> 00:06:13.205
organization needs. If it's a commodity, if it's a tool that exists

98
00:06:14.135 --> 00:06:19.575
third-party, off-the-shelf, it might be better to buy it rather than

99
00:06:19.575 --> 00:06:22.365
to spend the time and the money to build it.

100
00:06:24.255 --> 00:06:29.015
The third thing that we've seen companies do is that they rush

101
00:06:29.015 --> 00:06:33.615
to the cool solutions. For example, using generative AI when you

102
00:06:33.615 --> 00:06:37.845
really could use a rule engine or a filter.

103
00:06:39.175 --> 00:06:42.205
One of our clients, a construction company, a big construction

104
00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:44.285
company in the United States,

105
00:06:45.375 --> 00:06:49.615
they put out RFPs to different vendors, to subcontractors that come

106
00:06:49.615 --> 00:06:51.775
in and do the work as part of the projects.

107
00:06:51.775 --> 00:06:56.375
And they wanted to be able to score those vendors based on their

108
00:06:56.375 --> 00:07:00.375
past behavior. You know, were they on time?

109
00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:03.375
Were they on budget? Were there safety issues?

110
00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:05.565
Were there any insurance claims

111
00:07:07.015 --> 00:07:10.255
associated with them? All of these things they wanted to include

112
00:07:10.255 --> 00:07:15.415
into a score that would give them an idea of how well the vendor is

113
00:07:15.415 --> 00:07:19.935
doing in order to use that when they choose vendors for the new

114
00:07:19.935 --> 00:07:25.455
projects. And they wanted an AI tool that will score the vendors and

115
00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:26.565
give them recommendations.

116
00:07:27.975 --> 00:07:31.055
And when sitting with them and understanding the different pieces of

117
00:07:31.055 --> 00:07:33.935
data that they wanted as part of that score,

118
00:07:33.935 --> 00:07:36.135
we realized a simple rule engine would work.

119
00:07:36.135 --> 00:07:39.815
There was nothing very overly sophisticated about the way they

120
00:07:39.815 --> 00:07:42.215
wanted to score, which made sense for them.

121
00:07:42.215 --> 00:07:44.285
And so we recommended using

122
00:07:45.655 --> 00:07:49.975
a simpler solution and utilized AI for the forecasting and the

123
00:07:49.975 --> 00:07:54.215
prediction. And looking at it, we realized that we could look at how,

124
00:07:54.215 --> 00:07:58.295
given that vendor, we can predict how well they would possibly do on

125
00:07:58.295 --> 00:08:02.485
this new project given all of its details.

126
00:08:03.535 --> 00:08:08.897
And then the last mistake that organizations make has to do with

127
00:08:08.897 --> 00:08:13.537
data privacy. When you have public AI tools such as Claude,

128
00:08:13.537 --> 00:08:17.567
ChatGPT, etc. and confidential data in the organization,

129
00:08:18.057 --> 00:08:19.767
that is a recipe for disaster.

130
00:08:20.337 --> 00:08:23.777
That is a legal problem that is waiting to happen.

131
00:08:23.777 --> 00:08:28.977
So, data and AI governance is a must in these cases to understand

132
00:08:28.977 --> 00:08:32.897
exactly, provide guardrails, what data can be used,

133
00:08:32.897 --> 00:08:37.537
how it can be used, in what tools, where those tools are located,

134
00:08:37.537 --> 00:08:39.967
whether they are within the organization and within the sandbox.

135
00:08:43.217 --> 00:08:45.647
The third problem is a failed execution.

136
00:08:46.297 --> 00:08:47.417
So, the solution worked.

137
00:08:47.417 --> 00:08:49.577
You have the right problem, you have the right solution.

138
00:08:49.577 --> 00:08:50.767
Nobody's using it.

139
00:08:52.777 --> 00:08:57.007
Right? Your model could be 94% accurate, but nobody adopted it.

140
00:08:57.737 --> 00:09:01.327
Because it was buried somewhere in a portal that nobody opened.

141
00:09:01.937 --> 00:09:05.407
So, making it technically work is just part of the story.

142
00:09:05.897 --> 00:09:08.247
We also have to make sure that people use it.

143
00:09:09.057 --> 00:09:12.417
I claim that adoption is the only KPI that matters,

144
00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:15.647
right? A perfect model that nobody uses is a perfect failure.

145
00:09:16.337 --> 00:09:19.097
People have to know where it is, they have to use it,

146
00:09:19.097 --> 00:09:22.047
and it's important to embed it in the workflow

147
00:09:23.897 --> 00:09:25.537
that people are already doing.

148
00:09:25.537 --> 00:09:29.777
If they have to make a lot of changes, especially if they have to do

149
00:09:29.777 --> 00:09:33.847
more clicks in order to use the solution, they won't use it.

150
00:09:34.337 --> 00:09:38.817
Right? It needs to be as frictionless as possible as part of their

151
00:09:38.817 --> 00:09:43.297
workflow. One of the issues that we come across that we see a lot is

152
00:09:43.297 --> 00:09:47.137
that the business processes themselves are broken.

153
00:09:47.137 --> 00:09:51.257
They're not efficient. And so, just throwing AI at that workflow

154
00:09:51.257 --> 00:09:54.247
will just make the broken process work faster.

155
00:09:55.457 --> 00:09:57.657
But the solution may be to change the workflow,

156
00:09:57.657 --> 00:10:01.417
and this is where you can be innovative and make an impact by

157
00:10:01.417 --> 00:10:05.177
looking not only at the technical solution that may be required,

158
00:10:05.177 --> 00:10:08.817
but at the problem as a whole, at the team, and see if maybe the

159
00:10:08.817 --> 00:10:13.658
entire process can be changed, reimagined using AI tools that will

160
00:10:13.658 --> 00:10:14.568
make it frictionless.

161
00:10:16.258 --> 00:10:18.458
And the idea of, "Okay, well, we turned it on,"

162
00:10:18.458 --> 00:10:21.138
is not a go-live. Right?

163
00:10:21.138 --> 00:10:23.888
Go-live is when people start using the tool.

164
00:10:24.578 --> 00:10:27.898
When you see that the organization starts changing and actually

165
00:10:27.898 --> 00:10:30.128
using the tools.

166
00:10:31.978 --> 00:10:34.328
The fourth issue is no value measurement.

167
00:10:35.138 --> 00:10:38.618
So, you found a good problem, you fixed it, it works,

168
00:10:38.618 --> 00:10:40.858
you shipped it. You think it's making a difference in the

169
00:10:40.858 --> 00:10:42.488
organization, you have no idea.

170
00:10:43.298 --> 00:10:46.978
Because the one question that people in leadership will ask you is,

171
00:10:46.978 --> 00:10:49.658
"What's changed since we launched it?"

172
00:10:49.658 --> 00:10:53.538
Right? 6 months after go-live, you have no baseline,

173
00:10:53.538 --> 00:10:55.178
you can't answer that question.

174
00:10:55.178 --> 00:10:58.578
So, it's important when we think about these AI projects to think

175
00:10:58.578 --> 00:11:02.408
like product managers. That means that we need to first of all

176
00:11:03.178 --> 00:11:05.058
define a metric for success.

177
00:11:05.058 --> 00:11:07.168
Once we implement this AI solution,

178
00:11:08.218 --> 00:11:09.528
how do we know that it succeeds?

179
00:11:09.938 --> 00:11:12.938
Did it grow revenue? Did it reduce cost?

180
00:11:12.938 --> 00:11:16.448
Did it reduce risk? Did it increase customer satisfaction?

181
00:11:17.218 --> 00:11:18.448
Maybe employee satisfaction?

182
00:11:19.458 --> 00:11:22.898
There could be many different metrics that you decide on,

183
00:11:22.898 --> 00:11:26.178
but decide on a metric and then measure the baseline.

184
00:11:26.178 --> 00:11:28.088
What's the current state before AI?

185
00:11:28.778 --> 00:11:30.498
Does it take 45 minutes to do a task?

186
00:11:30.498 --> 00:11:33.338
Just like I talked about the letter creation tool.

187
00:11:33.338 --> 00:11:35.538
It took them days to create a letter.

188
00:11:35.538 --> 00:11:37.618
They had a lot of human errors.

189
00:11:37.618 --> 00:11:40.688
So, maybe you want to decrease the error rate.

190
00:11:41.418 --> 00:11:43.178
And then think about a threshold.

191
00:11:43.178 --> 00:11:45.818
What does it actually mean to work?

192
00:11:45.818 --> 00:11:46.718
What is success?

193
00:11:47.658 --> 00:11:52.258
So, if we reduce the time it takes to make those

194
00:11:52.258 --> 00:11:57.458
HR letters by 80%, that is a huge reduction that impacts the

195
00:11:57.458 --> 00:12:02.258
business. Because those HR representatives now have 80% of that time

196
00:12:02.258 --> 00:12:04.448
to do something else that is more efficient.

197
00:12:06.338 --> 00:12:10.768
And the last issue I want to talk about is failed communication.

198
00:12:11.818 --> 00:12:15.139
So, you picked the right problem, you did the right solution,

199
00:12:15.139 --> 00:12:19.419
it worked, you implemented it, you measured the value,

200
00:12:19.419 --> 00:12:20.769
and the value is real.

201
00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:26.299
Nobody heard about it. Nobody in the organization knows what an

202
00:12:26.299 --> 00:12:27.729
amazing job you did.

203
00:12:28.779 --> 00:12:32.979
Right? So, it's very important to also understand how to communicate

204
00:12:32.979 --> 00:12:37.259
success. The VP wants one sentence.

205
00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:40.819
Did the revenue go up? The mistakes that we make,

206
00:12:40.819 --> 00:12:44.019
what goes wrong in our communication, are things like we might

207
00:12:44.019 --> 00:12:48.419
present accuracy but not outcomes, not understand what the impact of

208
00:12:48.419 --> 00:12:50.089
the changes that we made was.

209
00:12:51.019 --> 00:12:55.099
Or we might use technical language addressing a business audience.

210
00:12:55.099 --> 00:12:58.379
They don't care about how well the model did,

211
00:12:58.379 --> 00:13:02.329
the AUC, etc., the precision, and so on.

212
00:13:03.699 --> 00:13:06.779
We don't show a before-and-after comparison saying this is what

213
00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:09.529
the impact actually did to demonstrate it.

214
00:13:10.179 --> 00:13:13.339
Nor do we give any detail beyond the fact that the model

215
00:13:13.339 --> 00:13:14.329
improved things.

216
00:13:15.979 --> 00:13:19.019
And finally, we may just send a report with some numbers rather than

217
00:13:19.019 --> 00:13:21.859
actually tell a story and build a narrative.

218
00:13:21.859 --> 00:13:27.499
And so, the way that we like to approach communicating around AI

219
00:13:27.499 --> 00:13:30.009
projects is to have a story.

220
00:13:30.699 --> 00:13:33.059
And say, first of all, what was the problem?

221
00:13:33.059 --> 00:13:35.899
The problem was that our analyst spent 3 hours a week on this report

222
00:13:35.899 --> 00:13:39.819
manually. And then we made a change, we built a tool that automates the

223
00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:44.329
extraction and flags anomalies in the document.

224
00:13:44.819 --> 00:13:49.099
And the result was that 2 and a half hours are saved per analyst per

225
00:13:49.099 --> 00:13:51.489
week, and errors were down 40%.

226
00:13:53.139 --> 00:13:55.219
And that's why Maria, our head of operations,

227
00:13:55.219 --> 00:13:58.899
you know, give it a name, make it personal, uses it every Monday at

228
00:13:58.899 --> 00:14:04.059
8:00 a.m. And our next steps are that we're expanding it to two more

229
00:14:04.059 --> 00:14:07.819
teams next quarter. Once you build a story and a narrative around

230
00:14:07.819 --> 00:14:12.059
it, it makes it easier for people to understand the value that these

231
00:14:12.059 --> 00:14:13.529
projects are bringing.

232
00:14:15.099 --> 00:14:18.251
So, to be successful, we need to solve the right problem first.

233
00:14:19.181 --> 00:14:22.341
You need to pick the right tool, not just the shiniest or the most

234
00:14:22.341 --> 00:14:25.701
convenient. Change management is not a phase.

235
00:14:25.701 --> 00:14:26.611
It's the product.

236
00:14:28.141 --> 00:14:32.091
You want to define the value before you build and measure it after.

237
00:14:32.941 --> 00:14:36.701
And you want to turn your results into a story that someone can

238
00:14:36.701 --> 00:14:42.221
tell. And now, we're going to go to our panelists and we're going to

239
00:14:42.221 --> 00:14:44.621
hear those stories. We're going to start with a little bit of

240
00:14:44.621 --> 00:14:49.101
history and understanding where we started and where we are today,

241
00:14:49.101 --> 00:14:52.491
and then hear some stories of success from different
